ce>Immunity.</source> <volume>18</volume>:<fpage>675</fpage>&#x02013;685.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">12753744</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib18"><label>18</label><citation citation-type="journal">Kitamoto, T., K. Takahashi, H. Takimoto, K. Tomizuka, M. Hayasaka, T. Tabira, and K. Hanaoka. <year>2005</year>. Functional redundancy of the Notch gene family during mouse embryogenesis: analysis of Notch gene expression in Notch3-deficient mice. <source>Biochem. Biophys. Res. Commun.</source> <volume>331</volume>:<fpage>1154</fpage>&#x02013;1162.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">15882997</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib19"><label>19</label><citation citation-type="journal">Krebs, L.T., Y. Xue, C.R. Norton, J.R. Shutter, M. Maguire, J.P. Sundberg, D. Gallahan, V. Closson, J. Kitajewski, R. Callahan, et al. <year>2000</year>. Notch signaling is essential for vascular morphogenesis in mice. <source>Genes Dev.</source> <volume>14</volume>:<fpage>1343</fpage>&#x02013;1352.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">10837027</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib20"><label>20</label><citation citation-type="journal">Tu, L., T.C. Fang, D. Artis, O. Shestova, S.E. Pross, I. Maillard, and W.S. Pear. <year>2005</year>. Notch signaling is an important regulator of type 2 immunity. <source>J. Exp. Med.</source> <volume>202</volume>:<fpage>1037</fpage>&#x02013;1042.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">16230473</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib21"><label>21</label><citation citation-type="journal">French, M.B., U. Koch, R.E. Shaye, M.A. McGill, S.E. Dho, C.J. Guidos, and C.J. McGlade. <year>2002</year>. Transgenic expression of numb inhibits notch signaling in immature thymocytes but does not alter T cell fate specification. <source>J. Immunol.</source> <volume>168</volume>:<fpage>3173</fpage>&#x02013;3180.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">11907069</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib22"><label>22</label><citation citation-type="journal">Hadland, B.K., N.R. Manley, D. Su, G.D. Longmore, C.L. Moore, M.S. Wolfe, E.H. Schroeter, and R. Kopan. <year>2001</year>. &#x003b3;-Secretase inhibitors repress thymocyte development. <source>Proc. Natl. Acad. Sci. USA.</source> <volume>98</volume>:<fpage>7487</fpage>&#x02013;7491.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">11416218</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib23"><label>23</label><citation citation-type="journal">Doerfler, P., M.S. Shearman, and R.M. Perlmutter. <year>2001</year>. Presenilin-dependent &#x003b3;-secretase activity modulates thymocyte development. <source>Proc. Natl. Acad. Sci. USA.</source> <volume>98</volume>:<fpage>9312</fpage>&#x02013;9317.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">11470902</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib24"><label>24</label><citation citation-type="journal">Deftos, M.L., E. Huang, E.W. Ojala, K.A. Forbush, and M.J. Bevan. <year>2000</year>. Notch1 signaling promotes the maturation of CD4 and CD8 SP thymocytes. <source>Immunity.</source> <volume>13</volume>:<fpage>73</fpage>&#x02013;84.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">10933396</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib25"><label>25</label><citation citation-type="journal">Bellavia, D., A.F. Campese, E. Alesse, A. Vacca, M.P. Felli, A. Balestri, A. Stoppacciaro, C. Tiveron, L. Tatangelo, M. Giovarelli, et al. <year>2000</year>. Constitutive activation of NF-kappa B and T-cell leukemia/lymphoma in Notch3 transgenic mice. <source>EMBO J.</source> <volume>19</volume>:<fpage>3337</fpage>&#x02013;3348.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">10880446</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib26"><label>26</label><citation citation-type="journal">Bray, S., and M. Furriols. <year>2001</year>. Notch pathway: making sense of suppressor of Hairless. <source>Curr. Biol.</source> <volume>11</volume>:<fpage>R217</fpage>&#x02013;R221.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">11301266</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib27"><label>27</label><citation citation-type="journal">Adler, S.H., E. Chiffoleau, L. Xu, N.M. Dalton, J.M. Burg, A.D. Wells, M.S. Wolfe, L.A. Turka, and W.S. Pear. <year>2003</year>. Notch signaling augments T c